course: dictionary
lesson: 7510
title: One word, many meanings
level: primary-4
stars: 0
explain: Some words have more than one meaning, and context tells you which one the writer means. The word potty is a good example. Read the words around it, and the clues will point you to the right meaning.
explain: «Mum bought a new potty, and Sam started potty training today.» Here a potty is a small plastic seat-bowl that a toddler uses as a toilet. The clues are new, Sam and training, because a small child is learning to use the toilet.
explain: «My grandma's gone potty, and she is dancing on the table!» Here potty means slightly crazy or silly. Nobody is talking about a toilet. Try your meaning in the sentence: "gone slightly crazy" still makes sense, so you have it right.
explain: Ask yourself: who is speaking, and what are the other words doing? When your first meaning does not fit, try the next one. If you are still unsure, check a dictionary.
fact: Potty is a noun (a potty, potties) and an adjective (potty, pottier, pottiest).
fact: As a noun, a potty is a small plastic seat-bowl that a toddler uses as a toilet while learning.
fact: «Go potty» means go to the toilet, and it is said to little children.
fact: In UK informal speech, «potty» can mean slightly crazy or silly, as in «My grandma's gone potty.»
fact: «Potty about» means very keen on something, as in «She's potty about horses.» A «potty mouth» is US slang for someone who swears a lot.
exercise: «Mum put the potty by the bath for little Sam, who is learning to use the toilet.» What is a potty in this sentence? | A type of small boat | *A small plastic seat-bowl for toilet training | A large cooking pot | A hairbrush
exercise: Ella says, «Grandad has gone potty and is singing to the cat.» What does potty mean? | Very happy | Using the toilet | Very quiet | *Slightly crazy or silly
exercise: «She's potty about horses.» What does potty about mean? | Scared of horses | *Very keen on horses | Angry with horses | Tired of horses
test: Jayden reads, «Go potty, sweetheart, then we can leave.» Who is this said to? | An adult at work | *A young child | A teacher | A dog
test: «The potty professor invented a machine that makes toast and sings.» What does potty mean here? | Very old | *Slightly crazy or silly | Very quiet | A toilet
exam: Which sentence uses potty to mean a toddler's small seat-bowl? | «My uncle has gone potty.» | «He's a potty mouth.» | «She's potty about football.» | *«The toddler sat on her potty.»
